Cell["7. What is here, and what is not", "Section",ExpressionUUID->"26d10c94-6cc8-1e49-9a16-49f416945ee4"],

Cell["\<\
Here: a system as rules and a state; an evolution as states, events and \
tokens; and five graphs over them. Four options. Two objects. That is the \
whole surface.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"8d9947d6-196c-df4f-849c-d2d7ea8e1238"],

Cell["\<\
Not here, deliberately, and the thing to weigh against the other stack: \
causal dependence through a shared vertex. In this stack a causal arrow is a \
hyperedge passing from one event to another, which is the standard reading in \
the graph-rewriting literature and in the Wolfram model. The other stack also \
offers a weaker reading, in which two events are related when their matches \
merely touch the same vertex. That is not a thing a token can express, and \
adding it would mean giving vertices tokens too. It is a real choice, not an \
oversight.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"303ddbd2-64d4-da4e-a8db-5c0e4a684604"],

Cell["\<\
Also not here: a states graph that merges across steps into a genuine \
multiway graph with shared tokens, and anything about causal invariance or \
confluence. Both are natural next steps if this stack is the one kept.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"9b4c6568-754f-de43-83d1-4a7a7cab808b"],

Cell["\<\
The evolution is a tree of state occurrences, not a graph: a state reached \
two ways is two occurrences, because which tokens it is made of depends on \
how it was reached. The states graph merges them for display, which is the \
right place to do it.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"18c4ec00-b4bd-3d48-ab16-cdffbbada2a8"]
},
